In Time Series:

What is t?

t = Current Time Period

For example:

| Date     | Value |
| -------- | ----: |
| 1/4/2017 |   230 |


Here,

t = 230

(Current value)

What is t-1?

t-1 = One time step before current value

| Date     | Value |
| -------- | ----: |
| 1/3/2017 |   215 |
| 1/4/2017 |   230 |

For 1/4/2017:

t   = 230
t-1 = 215

So t-1 = Lag 1.

=============

What is t-2?

t-2 = Two time steps before current value

| Date     | Value |
| -------- | ----: |
| 1/2/2017 |   220 |
| 1/3/2017 |   215 |
| 1/4/2017 |   230 |


For 1/4/2017:

t   = 230
t-1 = 215
t-2 = 220

So t-2 = Lag 2.

===========

| Date     | Value (t) | Value (t-1) | Value (t-2) |
| -------- | --------: | ----------: | ----------: |
| 1/4/2017 |       230 |         215 |         220 |
| 1/5/2017 |       235 |         230 |         215 |
| 1/6/2017 |       225 |         235 |         230 |


Example for 1/6/2017:

Current Value (t) = 225
Previous Day (t-1) = 235
Two Days Back (t-2) = 230

===========

Easy Real-Life Example

Suppose your daily expenses are:

| Day       | Expense |
| --------- | ------: |
| Monday    |     100 |
| Tuesday   |     120 |
| Wednesday |     150 |

For Wednesday:

t   = 150  (Today's expense)
t-1 = 120  (Yesterday's expense)
t-2 = 100  (Day before yesterday)

============

t    = Current value
t-1  = 1 step back (Lag 1)
t-2  = 2 steps back (Lag 2)
t-3  = 3 steps back (Lag 3)

t represents the current observation in a time series. t-1 is the previous observation (Lag 1), and t-2 is the observation two time periods
earlier (Lag 2). These lag values are used to study how past observations influence the current value.







